All four findings fixed and all gates green. No files were staged or committed.

**What changed**

1. **BLOCKER — `time.sleep` panicked.** `src/runtime.rs` now routes through `sleep_duration(seconds, span)`, which checks finite, non-negative, and bounded, then uses `Duration::try_from_secs_f64` (the same conversion returned as a `Result`) so nothing that would panic in `core` ever reaches `thread::sleep`. `time.sleep(-1)`, `-0.5`, `1e20` and beyond went from *"The interpreter thread stopped unexpectedly"* (uncatchable) to a named `Runtime` error a Redblue `try` can catch.

2. **`time.unix` did not panic** at the input the finding names — `time.unix("9999999999-01-01 00:00:00")` was already refused by `parse_from_str` with `Err`, and `DateTime::timestamp()` is `i64` arithmetic over a chrono-bounded year that cannot overflow. The probe did surface a real defect one step over: chrono parses year `-262143`, `time.unix` returned it as `-8334601228800`, and `time.format` refused that number. New `seconds_for_unix` applies the same window `seconds_for_format` uses, so the two builtins now agree.

3. **Three tests asserted nothing.** `say` returns `Nothing`, so matching `Ok(_)` passed whatever the value was — the pre-epoch test could not have failed on the `1970-01-01`-for-`1969-12-31` bug it exists to pin. The same pattern was in three more places. All now assert on the evaluated value; `eval_text` fails loudly on a non-text value.

**Red was measured, not assumed.** Reverting only the two production sites this round changed left the rest of the phase intact: 9 passed, 3 failed. Against the pre-phase tree, the strengthened date tests fail with `left: "1970-01-01", right: "1969-12-31"` — which is what they were blind to before.

**Gates**

| Gate | Result |
|---|---|
| `cargo fmt --all -- --check` | pass |
| `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` | pass, no `allow(` (clippy flagged one `manual_range_contains` in my new code; fixed as suggested, not suppressed) |
| `cargo test --all-targets` | 1083 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored (was 1075) |
| `rb test` | 340 run, 340 passed, 0 failed |

No test deleted, no `#[ignore]`, no skip, no gate weakened. 8 tests added, 3 rewritten.

Two things worth your attention:

- **The one-year `time.sleep` bound is a policy choice, not a panic threshold.** `try_from_secs_f64` accepts up to ~1.8e19 seconds. A program that once slept for a decade and blocked would now get an error instead of a block. It's recorded in the report's known gaps — say the word if you'd rather bound it at the actual conversion limit.
- **`rbops/verify.sh` still does not exist in this checkout**, so I ran its four documented constituents directly instead. I can't report a `verify.sh` result I didn't observe, and that row in the report still says so.
